cartridge "CRIBBAGE 128/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1988"

' CRIBBAGE 128
' A ColecoVision game based on "CRIBBAGE 128", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, August
' 1988 (Commodore 128, BASIC 7.0 with a machine-language helper, CR128,
' that shuffles, counts hands and chooses the computer's discards). The
' listing names no author (orig/cribbage128.bas, orig/cr128.prg and its
' disassembly orig/cr128.dis.txt).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: two-hand cribbage against the computer, to 121. The low cut
' deals first, then the deal alternates. Six cards each; each player lays
' two in the dealer's crib. The cut card (starter) is turned: a jack gives
' the dealer two for his heels. The play: the non-dealer leads, cards are
' laid in turn with the count kept to 31; a pair is 2, three of a kind 6,
' four 12, runs of three or more one a card (in any order), 15 and 31 two
' each; a player who cannot play says go and the other plays on, the last
' to play pegs one (none at 31), the count starts again; the last card of
' all pegs one. Then the hands are counted, the non-dealer first, then the
' dealer and the crib: fifteens 2, pairs 2, runs, a four-card flush 4 (5
' with the starter; the crib only with five), his nobs 1. First past 120
' wins; under 91 points the loser is skunked (double), under 61 double
' skunked (triple). The match table (match points, games won, hand, crib
' and pegging points) stays until the power is turned off.
'
' From the original, kept as the listing does it:
' - The computer's discard is the machine code's search ($169D): every
'   pair of the six, every starter rank 1-13 weighted by how many of that
'   rank are unseen, the kept hand counted (fifteens, pairs, runs, four-
'   card flush) plus one point a jack. With its own crib it adds what the
'   two discards and the starter make; with yours it subtracts the
'   routine's $1A7A byte (the first index of the best pair found so far,
'   kept from call to call) instead of the crib's value: a slip in the
'   listing, kept because it is the computer's play. Ties go to the later
'   pair. The hint (right button) is the same search from your side.
' - The computer's pegging is the listing's scoring of each card (lines
'   1750-1980 and 2150-2290, 3330-3590): a card that makes three of a
'   kind (30), a run (5 a card), 31 or 15 (11), a pair (7), and the
'   listing's small preferences on leads and counts, the first best card
'   in its hand order winning.
' - The counting, the pegging scores, his heels, the go, the last card,
'   the skunk rules and the match points (3 x, 2 x or 1 x the loser's
'   shortfall) and games won (3, 2 or 1) of the listing.
'
' Liberties:
' - Joystick and buttons instead of the keys: the stick chooses a card,
'   the left button lays it, the right button asks for a hint (before
'   your first discard). You cut for the deal with a press.
' - No name is asked: the player is YOU.
' - The shuffle is a new random shuffle (the listing's was a machine-code
'   riffle on the CIA timers); the deal is the listing's (computer six,
'   your six, then the starter).
' - Your cards are sorted by rank; the computer keeps its dealt order, as
'   in the listing (its hint and choices depend on it).
' - A cribbage board with pegs that hop along the holes; every count shows
'   what it is made of (fifteens, pairs, runs, flush, nobs).
' - The $1A7A byte starts at 0 at power on (on the C-128 it held whatever
'   was in memory).
' - Messages say "SKUNK" or "DOUBLE SKUNK" for either winner (the listing
'   skipped the double skunk message when the computer won).
' - The end of a game shows the table and the winner, then the title (no
'   ANOTHER GAME? question).
' - New sounds and an original title tune, "Fifteen Two".
'
' Controls: joystick left/right chooses a card, left button lays it, right
' button asks for a hint while discarding. Any button goes on after a
' count. Keypad not used. Title: up/down choose START, HOW TO PLAY (three
' pages) or CREDITS, a button picks.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune
' played once. `set sprite count` always given. Nothing flashes.

const TRk0 = 128
const TRk1 = 129
